Subject: [PATCH] Scene-scoped target resolution (editor cost + cross-scene name bleed)

config.goTargets is a single global list spanning every content scene, and
Capture()/Apply() walk all of it on every capture and every recall. For a
target in an unloaded scene that cost a slow GlobalObjectId lookup, a native
"Assertion failed: manager != NULL" with a managed stack-trace extraction,
and then a by-name fallback walking EVERY GameObject in memory. Measured:
283 such warnings (and 283 full scans) in one editor session.

The unscoped by-name fallback was also a correctness hole: several scenes
hold objects with the SAME name (ANCHOR_VR exists in three content scenes),
so a target of scene A could latch onto the homonym in scene B and receive
the wrong preset.

 - classify an anchor by the scene GUID carried in its GlobalObjectId and
   skip resolution entirely when that scene is not loaded (fails open);
 - restrict the by-name fallback to the anchor's own scene;
 - keep "not found" warnings for genuinely missing objects in LOADED scenes,
   stay silent for targets that are merely out of play.

Additive public surface only: TargetSceneUnloaded(string) and the
FindGOInScene(string, Scene) overload; FindGOInScene(string) is preserved.
Package: com.blindspot.presets 0.3.0 - Unity 6000.3.10f1
Rationale, decisions and measurements: see RAPPORT.md shipped alongside.
---
